window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-company-fs","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Financial Statements of a Company · Practice","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing Notes to Accounts on share capital as per Schedule III, a company must disclose the name and number of shares held by each shareholder holding more than what percentage of its shares?","image":"","options":["2%","5%","10%","25%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III requires disclosures that make clear who the ultimate owners of the company are.\nOne such disclosure is the shares held by each shareholder holding more than 5% of the shares, with the number of shares held.\nThe 10% and 25% options set the limit too high. Under those limits, significant owners would not be disclosed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"As per Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013, what is a reserve that is specifically represented by earmarked investments called?","image":"","options":["Provision","Surplus","Fund","Contingent liability"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schedule III adds a specific rule on disclosure of reserves: a reserve that is backed by earmarked investments shall be termed a 'Fund'.\nAn example is a Debenture Redemption Fund backed by Debenture Redemption Fund Investments.\n'Surplus' is the balance in the Statement of Profit and Loss, so it is not the right term.\nA provision is a charge for a known liability or loss. It is not a reserve backed by investments.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"If a requirement of an Accounting Standard conflicts with the presentation requirement of Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013, which one will prevail?","image":"","options":["Schedule III","The Accounting Standard","The one chosen by the Board of Directors","The one approved by shareholders in the AGM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"One of the presentation features of Schedule III is that Accounting Standards prevail over Schedule III.\nThis is also why terms used in Schedule III carry the meanings defined in the applicable Accounting Standards.\nNeither the directors nor the shareholders can choose which rule applies.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Under Schedule III, how is a debit balance in the Statement of Profit and Loss disclosed in the balance sheet of a company?","image":"","options":["Under 'Other non-current assets' as a fictitious asset","As a negative figure under the head 'Surplus' within Reserves and Surplus","As a deduction from Share Capital","Under 'Other current liabilities'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III requires a debit balance in the Statement of Profit and Loss to be shown as a negative figure under 'Surplus'.\nSurplus is part of Reserves and Surplus under Shareholders' Funds.\nIt is no longer shown on the assets side as a fictitious asset, which is the tempting option.\nIt is not deducted from Share Capital, and it is not a liability.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the Statement of Profit and Loss as per Schedule III is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For a company other than a finance company, dividend income is shown under 'Other income'","Bank charges are shown under 'Finance costs' along with interest on borrowings","For a finance company, interest and dividend income form part of 'Revenue from operations'","Amortisation means writing off the amount relating to intangible assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option 1 is correct: dividend income is other income for a non-finance company.\nOption 2 is incorrect: only interest cost is shown under Finance costs. Bank charges go under 'Other expenses'.\nOption 3 is correct: for a finance company, interest and dividend are operating revenue.\nOption 4 is correct: this is how NCERT defines amortisation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Going concern postulate\n(B) Money measurement postulate\n(C) Realisation postulate\n(D) Materiality convention\nList-II\n(i) Revenue is included in the sales of the year of sale even if the price is received over several years\n(ii) The enterprise is assumed to exist for a long period, so assets are shown at historical cost\n(iii) Small items like postage stamps are charged as expense in the year of purchase\n(iv) The value of money is assumed to remain the same, so assets bought in different years are shown at the amounts paid","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): going concern assumes a long life for the business, so assets are shown at historical cost.\nB-(iv): money measurement assumes the value of money stays the same over time, so assets keep the amounts paid for them.\nC-(i): realisation puts revenue in the year in which the sale is made.\nD-(iii): materiality allows small asset-like items to be treated as expenses.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Items of a company) with List-II (Heads/Sub-heads of the Balance Sheet as per Schedule III):\nList-I\n(A) Trade receivables expected to be realised 15 months after the reporting date (beyond the operating cycle)\n(B) Investment expected to be realised within 8 months\n(C) Loose tools\n(D) Instalment of a 5-year term loan falling due within the next 12 months\nList-II\n(i) Other current liabilities\n(ii) Inventories\n(iii) Other non-current assets\n(iv) Current investments","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Trade receivables realised beyond 12 months or the operating cycle are shown as 'Other non-current assets' (iii), with a note.\n(B) Investments expected to be realised within 12 months are Current investments (iv).\n(C) Loose tools are shown under Inventories (ii), as in Illustration 2.\n(D) The current maturity of a long-term loan is shown under Other current liabilities (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company's balance sheet and statement of profit and loss give no idea about the strained relations between its management and workers, or about the falling quality of work on the shop floor. Which limitation of financial statements does this show?","image":"","options":["Aggregate information","No qualitative information","Bias","Assets may not realise"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: financial statements contain only monetary information.\nThey leave out qualitative information such as industrial relations, labour relations, industrial climate and quality of work.\nStrained worker relations and falling work quality are qualitative facts, so they never appear in the statements.\nAggregate information is the tempting wrong option. That limitation is about figures being shown in totals rather than in detail. It is not about non-monetary facts being left out.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"As per Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013, in which format must a company present its balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Horizontal (T-shaped) format","Vertical format","Either horizontal or vertical, at the company's choice","Account form with debit and credit sides"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III prescribes a vertical format for presenting the balance sheet (Exhibit 3.1).\nIn this format, Equity and Liabilities are shown first and Assets below them.\nThe horizontal or T-shaped account form is the format that has been replaced, so options (1) and (4) are wrong.\nThe company has no choice in the matter because the format is prescribed, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (accounting practice) with List-II (aspect of the nature of financial statements):\nList-I\n(A) Inventory valued at cost or market price, whichever is lower\n(B) Creating a provision for doubtful debts on estimates\n(C) Revenue included in the year of sale though cash is received over several years\n(D) Assets bought at different times shown at the amounts paid for them\nList-II\n(i) Personal judgements\n(ii) Recorded facts (historical cost)\n(iii) Accounting conventions\n(iv) Realisation postulate","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The cost-or-market-whichever-lower rule is an accounting convention, so A-(iii). \n(B) Provisions for doubtful debts rest on estimates, i.e. personal judgements, so B-(i). \n(C) Recognising revenue in the year of sale is the realisation postulate, so C-(iv). \n(D) Showing assets at the original amounts paid reflects recorded facts based on historical cost, so D-(ii). \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the statements about the nature of financial statements.\nStatement I: Stationery is valued at cost, not at cost or market price, whichever is lower.\nStatement II: Small items such as pens, pencils and postage stamps are treated as expenses in the year of purchase, even though they are assets in nature, because of the convention of materiality.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT states that stationery is valued at cost. It is not valued on the 'cost or market price, whichever is less' principle, which applies to inventory.\nStatement II: Correct. Under the convention of materiality, small items like pens and postage stamps are charged as expenses in the year they are bought, even though they are assets in nature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match the items of a manufacturing company in List-I with the heads of the Statement of Profit and Loss in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Dividend received on shares held as investment\n(B) Leave encashment paid to staff\n(C) Interest on 9% debentures\n(D) Patents written off\nList-II\n(i) Finance costs\n(ii) Depreciation and amortisation expense\n(iii) Other income\n(iv) Employee benefits expense","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Dividend income of a non-finance company is shown under Other income, so A-(iii).\n(B) Leave encashment is a staff-related cost, shown under Employee benefits expense, so B-(iv).\n(C) Interest on borrowings such as debentures is shown under Finance costs, so C-(i).\n(D) Writing off an intangible asset like patents is amortisation, shown under Depreciation and amortisation expense, so D-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following companies is NOT required to prepare its balance sheet in the form given in Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013?","image":"","options":["A listed manufacturing company","A private limited trading company","A banking company","A public company engaged in providing IT services"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schedule III applies to all Indian companies that prepare financial statements under it.\nIt does not apply to insurance or banking companies.\nIt also does not apply to any company whose form of balance sheet is specified under another Act.\nA banking company is therefore excluded.\nManufacturing, trading and service companies, whether public or private, must follow Schedule III.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Ruchi Ltd. holds loose tools worth ₹25,000 at the end of the year. As per Schedule III of the Companies Act, 2013, under which head and sub-head will loose tools be shown in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Non-current assets – Fixed assets (Tangible assets)","Current assets – Inventories","Current assets – Other current assets","Non-current assets – Other non-current assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Schedule III treats all inventories as current. Loose tools are included under inventories, as in NCERT's illustrations of Amba Ltd. and Shine and Bright Ltd.\nWhy option (1) fails: loose tools are physical items, but they are not grouped with tangible fixed assets such as machinery or vehicles.\nWhy options (3) and (4) fail: 'Other current assets' and 'Other non-current assets' are residual heads. They are not used when a specific head like Inventories applies.\nSo loose tools appear under Current assets → Inventories.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Shares of a company have been forfeited and not yet reissued. In the notes to accounts on share capital, how is the share forfeiture amount treated?","image":"","options":["It is deducted from subscribed but not fully paid capital at the face value of the forfeited shares.","It is added to subscribed capital at the amount originally paid up on the forfeited shares.","It is shown under Reserves and Surplus as Capital Reserve.","It is shown as a current liability because it may be refunded."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III requires forfeited shares to be disclosed at the amount originally paid up on them.\nIn the notes, this Share Forfeiture balance is added to subscribed capital, as shown in the Dinkar Ltd illustration.\nIt goes to Capital Reserve only when the forfeited shares are reissued, and then only the surplus is transferred. So option (3) does not apply here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items will be classified as CURRENT in the balance sheet of a company as per Schedule III?\n(i) Investments expected to be realised within twelve months\n(ii) Deferred tax assets (net)\n(iii) Trade receivables to be realised after 15 months from the date of recognition (operating cycle 12 months)\n(iv) Cash and cash equivalents\n(v) A provision to be settled within 12 months from the balance sheet date\n(vi) Share application money pending allotment (non-refundable, within issued capital)\n(vii) Stock-in-trade","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Current: current investments.\n(ii) Non-current: deferred tax assets are always non-current.\n(iii) Non-current: shown under Other non-current assets.\n(iv) Current: cash and cash equivalents are always current.\n(v) Current: shown as a short-term provision.\n(vi) Non-current: as stated in Schedule III.\n(vii) Current: inventories are always current.\nSo 4 items are current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Sumit Ltd. received Rs. 30,000 as calls in advance from some of its shareholders. Under which major head and sub-head will this amount appear in the balance sheet prepared as per Schedule III?","image":"","options":["Current Liabilities – Other current liabilities","Shareholders' Funds – Share capital","Non-current Liabilities – Other long-term liabilities","Shareholders' Funds – Reserves and surplus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Calls in advance is money received before the company has made the call. Until the call is made, the company owes this amount to the shareholders.\nIt is a liability. It is not part of share capital, because share capital includes only called-up amounts.\nIt is adjusted against the next call, which is due within the operating cycle. So it is shown under Current Liabilities → Other current liabilities.\nOption (2) is tempting, but the amount has not yet been called, so it cannot be added to share capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are required by Schedule III to be disclosed in the notes to accounts relating to share capital?\n(i) Number of shares held by each shareholder holding more than 5% shares\n(ii) Shares held by the holding company or ultimate holding company\n(iii) Reconciliation of the number of shares outstanding at the beginning and end of the period\n(iv) Market price of the shares on the stock exchange at the year-end\n(v) Forfeited shares (amount originally paid up)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Required: this disclosure shows the major owners.\n(ii) Required: shares held by the holding or ultimate holding company must be disclosed.\n(iii) Required: the opening and closing number of shares must be reconciled for each class.\n(iv) Not required: market price is not part of the share capital disclosure, which is based on par value.\n(v) Required: forfeited shares are shown at the amount originally paid up.\nSo 4 items are required.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Sahyog Finance Ltd. is a finance company. It earned interest on loans given and dividend on its investments during the year. In its Statement of Profit and Loss, these incomes will be shown under:","image":"","options":["Other income","Exceptional items","Revenue from operations","Extraordinary items"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For a finance company, revenue from operations includes revenue from interest, dividend and income from other financial services. Earning interest and dividend is its main business.\nFor a company other than a finance company, interest and dividend would go under 'Other income'. This is why option (1) is the tempting trap here.\nThese incomes arise regularly, so they are not exceptional or extraordinary items.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of financial statements as described in NCERT?\n(i) They are based on historical cost and do not reflect the current situation\n(ii) They may be affected by the personal bias of accountants\n(iii) They form the basis for granting credit\n(iv) They contain no qualitative information such as labour relations\n(v) They disclose significant accounting policies followed","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Limitation: assets are shown at historical cost, not current value.\n(ii) Limitation: personal judgements can introduce bias.\n(iii) Not a limitation: this is a use or importance of financial statements.\n(iv) Limitation: they contain only monetary information.\n(v) Not a limitation: this is an objective of financial statements.\nSo the number of limitations is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The notes to accounts must disclose the aggregate number and class of shares bought back during the period of 5 years immediately preceding the balance sheet date.\nStatement II: Shares allotted as fully paid bonus shares need to be disclosed only if they were allotted during the current accounting year.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Shares bought back must be disclosed for the 5 years immediately preceding the balance sheet date.\nStatement II is wrong. Bonus shares allotted as fully paid are covered by the same 5-year disclosure rule, not just the current year. Shares allotted for consideration other than cash also follow this 5-year rule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An insurance company is not required to present its balance sheet in the form prescribed by Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013.\nReason (R): Schedule III prescribes a horizontal format for presenting the balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Schedule III does not apply to insurance or banking companies.\nIt also does not apply to companies whose format is specified under another Act.\nR is false. Schedule III prescribes a vertical format, not a horizontal one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under the convention of materiality, small items like pens, pencils and postage stamps are treated as expenditure in the year of purchase, even though they are assets in nature.\nStatement II: Under the realisation postulate, revenue from a sale is included only in the year in which the full sale price is received.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: materiality allows small asset-type items to be charged as expenditure in the year they are bought.\nStatement II is incorrect: the realisation postulate records revenue in the year the sale is made, even if the sale price is received over several years.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: Money received against share warrants is shown as a separate line item under 'Shareholders' Funds' in the balance sheet.\nStatement II: Share warrants are instruments issued against money received by the company, and that money is converted into shares at a specified date at a specified rate.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Schedule III lists 'Money received against share warrants' as item (c) under Shareholders' Funds, as a separate line item.\nStatement II is correct. The money is received now and converted into shares later, at a specified date and at a specified rate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situations) with List-II (limitations of financial statements):\nList-I\nA. Trade receivables of ₹ 4 crore are shown as one figure, with no customer-wise details\nB. Two accountants charge different depreciation on identical machines, using their own estimates of useful life\nC. In a forced liquidation, machinery fetches far less than its book value\nD. Land bought 15 years ago is still shown at its purchase price\nList-II\n1. Do not reflect current situation\n2. Assets may not realise\n3. Bias\n4. Aggregate information","image":"","options":["A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1","A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-4: a single total figure without details is aggregate information.\nB-3: depreciation that depends on personal estimates of useful life brings bias into the results.\nC-2: book value not being recovered on forced liquidation means assets may not realise their stated values.\nD-1: historical cost ignores changes in purchasing power, so the figure does not reflect the current situation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Some assets shown in the balance sheet of a company may fetch much less than their stated values if the company is forced into liquidation.\nReason (R): Assets shown in the balance sheet merely reflect their unexpired or unamortised cost and not their realisable value.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Accounts follow conventions such as going concern and historical cost, so stated asset values may not be realised in a forced liquidation.\nR is true. NCERT says assets in the balance sheet reflect merely unexpired or unamortised cost.\nR explains A. Because the figures are book costs and not realisable values, the stated amounts may not be recovered on liquidation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The following balances relate to Vibrant Ltd. as at 31st March 2017:\n• Term loan Rs. 10,00,000, repayable in 4 equal annual instalments, the first due within the next 12 months\n• Bank overdraft Rs. 50,000\n• Sundry creditors (payable within 3 months) Rs. 1,20,000\n• Provision for tax (payable within the year) Rs. 30,000\n• Deferred tax liability Rs. 40,000\n• Proposed dividend for 2016–17 (yet to be approved by shareholders) Rs. 1,00,000\n• 12% Debentures redeemable after 5 years Rs. 5,00,000\n• Interest accrued but not due on debentures Rs. 15,000\nWhat is the total of 'Current Liabilities' in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,65,000","Rs. 5,65,000","Rs. 5,05,000","Rs. 2,15,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Current maturity of the term loan = 10,00,000 ÷ 4 = Rs. 2,50,000 (Other current liabilities).\nAdd: bank overdraft 50,000 (short-term borrowings), creditors 1,20,000 (trade payables), provision for tax 30,000 (short-term provisions) and interest accrued 15,000 (other current liabilities).\nExcluded: deferred tax liability (always non-current), proposed dividend (contingent liability in notes) and debentures (long-term borrowings).\nTotal = 2,50,000 + 50,000 + 1,20,000 + 30,000 + 15,000 = Rs. 4,65,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Before revising corporate tax rates, the Ministry of Finance studies the profits reported by companies over several years. Which use of financial statements is illustrated here?","image":"","options":["Guide to the value of investment already made","Report on stewardship function","Basis for fiscal policies","Aids trade associations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The government's fiscal policies, especially taxation policies, depend on the financial performance of companies.\nFinancial statements provide the basic input for industrial, taxation and other economic policies.\nThe stewardship function is about reporting to owners, not to the government, so option (2) does not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company sold machinery on instalments in 2024-25 for Rs 6,00,000. The price will be received over three years, but the full sale is included in the revenue of 2024-25. Which postulate supports this treatment?","image":"","options":["Going concern postulate","Money measurement postulate","Accounting period postulate","Realisation postulate"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: under the realisation postulate, revenue is included in the sales of the year in which the sale took place. This holds even if the sale price is received over several years.\nWhy (4) fits: the sale was made in 2024-25, so the whole Rs 6,00,000 is revenue of that year.\nWhy going concern fails: going concern explains why assets are shown at historical cost, not when revenue is recognised.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are shown under 'Other income' (not 'Revenue from operations') in the Statement of Profit and Loss of a manufacturing company?\n(i) Interest income\n(ii) Dividend income\n(iii) Sale of services\n(iv) Net gain on sale of investments\n(v) Other operating revenues","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Interest income: Other income, since this is not a finance company. \n(ii) Dividend income: Other income. \n(iii) Sale of services: Revenue from operations. \n(iv) Net gain on sale of investments: Other income. \n(v) Other operating revenues: Revenue from operations. \nSo three items, (i), (ii) and (iv), are Other income. For a finance company, interest and dividend would instead be revenue from operations. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"From the following balances of Raghav Ltd., calculate the amount to be shown under 'Inventories' in its balance sheet:\nStock-in-trade Rs. 2,50,000; Loose tools Rs. 40,000; Stores and spares Rs. 25,000; Motor vehicles Rs. 3,00,000; Bills receivable Rs. 60,000","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 2,90,000","Rs. 3,15,000","Rs. 3,75,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: stock-in-trade Rs. 2,50,000, loose tools Rs. 40,000, stores and spares Rs. 25,000.\nInventories include stock-in-trade, loose tools, and stores and spares.\nMotor vehicles are tangible fixed assets, and bills receivable are trade receivables, so both are excluded.\nCalculation: 2,50,000 + 40,000 + 25,000 = Rs. 3,15,000\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The Board of Directors of Kavya Ltd. proposed a final dividend of Rs. 3,00,000 for the year 2016–17 after preparing its accounts. The Annual General Meeting will be held in July 2017. How will this proposed dividend be shown in the balance sheet as at 31st March, 2017?","image":"","options":["Under Short-term provisions in Current Liabilities","Under Other current liabilities","As a contingent liability in Notes to Accounts","As a deduction from Surplus under Reserves and Surplus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Proposed dividend becomes payable only when shareholders declare (approve) it at the AGM. That meeting falls in the next financial year.\nShareholders may also reduce the proposed amount, so the obligation is uncertain.\nAS-4 therefore requires it to be shown as a contingent liability in Notes to Accounts.\nIt is not a provision or a current liability on the face of the balance sheet. It will be accounted for only after it is declared.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company has forfeited some shares and has not yet reissued them. How is the balance of Share Forfeiture Account presented in the balance sheet as per Schedule III?","image":"","options":["Added to subscribed capital in the Notes to Accounts on Share Capital","Shown as Capital Reserve under Reserves and Surplus","Shown under Other Current Liabilities","Disclosed as a contingent liability in the Notes to Accounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The forfeited amount is the money originally paid up by the defaulting shareholders. It stays with the company.\nIn the Share Capital note, this amount is added to subscribed capital. For example, Dinkar Ltd adds 200 × ₹80 = ₹16,000.\nIt becomes Capital Reserve only after the forfeited shares are reissued. Until then, option (2) is wrong.\nThe company owes nothing on this amount, so it is neither a liability nor a contingent liability.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The following balances appear in the books of Meher Ltd. as on 31st March, 2017:\nSecurities premium Rs. 50,000\nPreliminary expenses (incurred during the year) Rs. 80,000\nStatement of Profit and Loss (credit balance) Rs. 2,00,000\nWhat amount will be shown as 'Reserves and Surplus' in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,70,000","Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 3,30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: securities premium Rs. 50,000, preliminary expenses Rs. 80,000, Statement of Profit and Loss credit balance Rs. 2,00,000.\nRule: preliminary expenses are written off fully in the year they are incurred, first from securities premium and the balance from the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nWorking: 50,000 − 80,000 = (30,000); then (30,000) + 2,00,000 = 1,70,000.\nSo Reserves and Surplus = Rs. 1,70,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"On April 1, 2017, Jumbo Ltd. issued 10,000, 12% debentures of Rs. 100 each at a discount of 20%, redeemable after 5 years. The company decided to write off the entire discount on March 31, 2018.\nAssertion (A): In the balance sheet prepared immediately after the issue, the discount of Rs. 2,00,000 is shown under 'Other current assets'.\nReason (R): The discount will be written off within twelve months of the balance sheet date.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Discount = 10,000 × Rs. 100 × 20% = Rs. 2,00,000.\nThe discount is a borrowing cost that will be written off on March 31, 2018. That is within 12 months of the balance sheet date.\nAn item to be realised or settled within 12 months is classified as current. So the discount appears under Other current assets, just as in NCERT's Amba Ltd. illustration. A is true.\nR states the 12-month write-off, which is exactly the reason for this classification. So R is true and correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nAssertion (A): Shares of another company bought by a firm with the intention of selling them within the next 6 months are shown as current investments under current assets.\nReason (R): As per Schedule III, all inventories are always treated as current assets.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Investments expected to be realised within twelve months are classified as current investments under current assets. Shares meant to be sold within 6 months meet this test.\nR is true. Schedule III treats all inventories as current.\nHowever, R does not explain A. Investments are not inventories. Their current classification comes from the twelve-month realisation test, not from the rule for inventories.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: In February 2017, Meghna Ltd. lost its biggest export market. Its long-term supply agreement with a major buyer was also cancelled. Its balance sheet as at 31st March, 2017 shows no trace of either event, although both will seriously affect the company. Which limitation of financial statements is illustrated?","image":"","options":["Bias","Aggregate information","Do not reflect current situation","Vital information missing"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the balance sheet does not disclose information on loss of markets and cessation of agreements.\nSuch events have a vital bearing on the enterprise.\nThe case describes exactly these two events, so the limitation is 'vital information missing'.\n'Do not reflect current situation' is a different limitation. It refers to assets being shown at historical cost, not to business events being left out.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Navrang Traders Ltd. (not a finance company) for the year ended 31st March, compute the Profit before tax as per Schedule III:\nRevenue from operations (net sales) ₹12,00,000\nInterest received on investments ₹30,000\nPurchases of stock-in-trade ₹5,00,000\nOpening stock-in-trade ₹80,000; Closing stock-in-trade ₹1,20,000\nSalaries and wages ₹1,50,000\n10% Debentures ₹2,00,000 (issued on 1st April of the same year)\nBank charges ₹5,000\nDepreciation ₹40,000\nRent ₹25,000","image":"","options":["₹4,50,000","₹5,50,000","₹5,30,000","₹5,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Total revenue = Revenue from operations + Other income = 12,00,000 + 30,000 = ₹12,30,000.\nChange in inventories = Opening − Closing = 80,000 − 1,20,000 = −₹40,000. This reduces expenses.\nFinance cost = 10% of 2,00,000 = ₹20,000. Bank charges (5,000) and rent (25,000) go to Other expenses = ₹30,000.\nTotal expenses = 5,00,000 − 40,000 + 1,50,000 + 20,000 + 40,000 + 30,000 = ₹7,00,000.\nSo Profit before tax = 12,30,000 − 7,00,000 = ₹5,30,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Item) with List-II (Presentation as per Schedule III):\nList-I\n(A) Calls in arrears\n(B) Share forfeiture (shares not reissued)\n(C) Securities premium\n(D) Money received against share warrants\nList-II\n(i) Separate line item under Shareholders' Funds\n(ii) Deducted from called-up capital in the Share Capital note\n(iii) Under Reserves and Surplus\n(iv) Added to subscribed capital in the Share Capital note\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(ii): Calls in arrears are amounts called but unpaid, so they are deducted from called-up capital.\nB-(iv): The forfeited amount (the money originally paid up) is added to subscribed capital.\nC-(iii): Securities premium is listed under Reserves and Surplus.\nD-(i): Money received against share warrants is a separate line item under Shareholders' Funds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Financial statements help reveal the gap between management performance and ownership expectations.\nStatement II: Financial statements alone make up the entire annual report of a company.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Reporting management's performance to the owners is the stewardship function of financial statements.\nStatement II is incorrect. Financial statements are an integral part of the annual report, but not the whole of it. The report also contains the directors' report, auditors' report, corporate governance report, and management discussion and analysis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are shown under the sub-head 'Reserves and Surplus' in a company's balance sheet as per Schedule III?\nCapital Reserve; Securities Premium; Calls in advance; Debenture Redemption Reserve; Forfeited shares account; Revaluation Reserve; Money received against share warrants","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Capital Reserve: under Reserves and Surplus.\nSecurities Premium: under Reserves and Surplus.\nDebenture Redemption Reserve: under Reserves and Surplus.\nRevaluation Reserve: under Reserves and Surplus.\nCalls in advance: not included, it goes to Other current liabilities.\nForfeited shares account: not included, it is added to Share Capital (as in the Dinkar Ltd. note).\nMoney received against share warrants: not included, it is a separate line item under Shareholders' Funds.\nTotal under Reserves and Surplus = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A trade association of textile manufacturers collects the published financial statements of its member companies. Which of the following is it most likely to do with this information?","image":"","options":["Fix the market price of members' shares","Develop standard ratios and design a uniform system of accounts","Decide the rate of income tax applicable to members","Sanction loans to member companies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says trade associations analyse financial statements to provide service and protection to their members.\nFor this purpose they may develop standard ratios and design a uniform system of accounts.\nThe other options are not their role:\n- Share prices are quoted by stock brokers.\n- Tax rates are set by the government.\n- Loans are granted by banks.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"As per Schedule III of the Companies Act, 2013, under which head are Deferred tax liabilities (net) always shown in the balance sheet of a company?","image":"","options":["Current Liabilities","Non-current Liabilities","Shareholders' Funds","Contingent liabilities in Notes to Accounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under Schedule III, deferred tax assets and liabilities are always classified as non-current.\nDeferred tax liabilities (net) therefore appear as sub-head (b) under Non-current Liabilities.\nThey are never treated as current, even if part of the amount may reverse within 12 months.\nThey are not a contingent liability and not part of shareholders' funds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Item) with List-II (Sub-head in the balance sheet as per Schedule III):\nList-I\n(A) Loose tools\n(B) Bills receivable\n(C) Goodwill\n(D) Provision for tax\nList-II\n(I) Short-term provisions\n(II) Intangible assets\n(III) Inventories\n(IV) Trade receivables","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Loose tools are shown under Inventories (III).\n(B) Bills receivable are shown under Trade receivables (IV).\n(C) Goodwill is a fixed asset with no physical form, so it goes under Intangible assets (II).\n(D) Provision for tax is shown under Short-term provisions in current liabilities (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"On 1st April 2016, Sarthak Ltd. took a term loan of Rs. 12,00,000 from a bank. The loan is repayable in 6 equal annual instalments, with the first instalment due on 31st March 2018. What amount will be shown under 'Long-term borrowings' in the balance sheet as at 31st March 2017?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,00,000","Rs. 10,00,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 8,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: loan outstanding = Rs. 12,00,000; 6 equal instalments, so each instalment = Rs. 2,00,000.\nRule: the current maturity of long-term debt (the amount repayable within 12 months) is shown under Other current liabilities.\nThe instalment due on 31-3-2018 falls within 12 months, so Rs. 2,00,000 goes to Other current liabilities.\nLong-term borrowings = 12,00,000 − 2,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the balance sheet of a company prepared as per Schedule III:\nStatement I: Calls in advance received from shareholders are shown under 'Current Liabilities' in the sub-head 'Other current liabilities'.\nStatement II: Unpaid (unclaimed) dividend is shown under 'Current Liabilities' in the sub-head 'Short-term provisions'.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Calls in advance are money received before a call is made. The company owes this money to shareholders until the call falls due, so it is shown under Other current liabilities. It is not share capital.\nStatement II is incorrect. Unpaid dividend has already been declared and is a definite amount payable. It is not an estimated provision, so it is shown under Other current liabilities, not Short-term provisions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a ground for classifying an item as 'current' in a company's balance sheet as per Schedule III?","image":"","options":["It is held primarily for the purpose of trading","It is expected to be realised or settled within twelve months","It is cash or a cash equivalent","It is a fixed asset whose useful life is less than twelve months"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct ground: items held primarily for trading are current.\n(2) Correct ground: items expected to be realised or settled within twelve months are current.\n(3) Correct ground: cash and cash equivalents are always current.\n(4) Not a ground: NCERT states that fixed assets remain non-current even if their useful life is less than 12 months.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Financial statements are an integral part of a company's annual report. Which of the following is NOT one of the other components of the annual report mentioned in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Directors' report","Auditors' report","Prospectus inviting public subscription","Management discussion and analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT says the annual report contains the financial statements along with:\n- the directors' report\n- the auditors' report\n- the corporate governance report\n- management discussion and analysis.\nA prospectus is a separate document. It is issued when inviting the public to subscribe for securities, so it is not part of the annual report.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items will be shown under 'Other income' in the Statement of Profit and Loss of a manufacturing company?\n(i) Interest on fixed deposits\n(ii) Dividend received on shares held\n(iii) Net gain on sale of investments\n(iv) Sale of services\n(v) Salaries paid\n(vi) Interest on debentures issued by the company","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Other income: interest income of a non-finance company.\n(ii) Other income: dividend income.\n(iii) Other income: net gain on sale of investments.\n(iv) Not other income: it is part of revenue from operations.\n(v) Not other income: it is an employee benefits expense.\n(vi) Not other income: it is a finance cost.\nSo 3 items qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the statements regarding investments:\nStatement I: Investments expected to be realised within twelve months are classified as current investments under current assets.\nStatement II: Non-current investments are disclosed only in the Notes to Accounts and not on the face of the balance sheet.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Investments expected to realise within 12 months are current investments; all others are non-current investments.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that both current and non-current investments are shown on the face of the balance sheet, with details given in the notes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"An accounts trainee classified the following items while preparing a balance sheet as per Schedule III:\n(i) Loan repayable on demand: Short-term borrowings\n(ii) Trade payables to be settled 18 months after the balance sheet date: Other long-term liabilities\n(iii) Machinery with a useful life of 10 months: Current assets\n(iv) Investment expected to be realised within 9 months: Current investments\n(v) Instalment of a long-term loan repayable within the next 12 months: Long-term borrowings\n(vi) Debit balance of Statement of Profit and Loss: Negative figure under 'Surplus' in Reserves and Surplus\nHow many of the above classifications are correct?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: loans repayable on demand are short-term borrowings.\n(ii) Correct: trade payables settled beyond 12 months go under other long-term liabilities.\n(iii) Incorrect: fixed assets stay non-current even if their useful life is under 12 months.\n(iv) Correct: investments realisable within 12 months are current investments.\n(v) Incorrect: current maturities of a long-term loan go under other current liabilities.\n(vi) Correct: a debit balance is shown as a negative figure under Surplus.\nCorrect count = 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"On 1 April, Sagar Ltd. issued 8,000, 11% debentures of ₹100 each at a discount of 10%, redeemable after 5 years. The company decided to write off the full discount on 31 March of the same financial year. What will be the total of the balance sheet prepared immediately after the issue of these debentures?","image":"","options":["₹7,20,000","₹8,00,000","₹8,80,000","₹8,88,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 8,000 debentures × ₹100 = ₹8,00,000 face value, issued at a 10% discount.\nCash received = 8,00,000 × 90% = ₹7,20,000. Discount = ₹80,000.\nEquity and liabilities side: Long-term borrowings (11% debentures) = ₹8,00,000.\nAssets side: Cash and cash equivalents ₹7,20,000. The discount of ₹80,000 will be written off within 12 months, so it is shown under Other current assets.\nTotal = 7,20,000 + 80,000 = ₹8,00,000.\nNo interest is due yet immediately after issue.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"At the year-end, Kavya Ltd. has the following items:\nClosing inventory of goods: cost Rs. 2,40,000; market price Rs. 2,10,000\nUnused stationery: cost Rs. 15,000; market price Rs. 12,000\nFollowing the conventions described in NCERT for valuing these items, what is the combined value at which they will be shown in the financial statements?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,22,000","Rs. 2,25,000","Rs. 2,55,000","Rs. 2,52,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: inventory cost Rs. 2,40,000 and market price Rs. 2,10,000. Stationery cost Rs. 15,000 and market price Rs. 12,000.\nRule 1: Inventory is valued at cost or market price, whichever is lower. Here that is Rs. 2,10,000.\nRule 2: NCERT states that stationery is valued at cost, not at the lower of cost or market price. Here that is Rs. 15,000.\nTotal = 2,10,000 + 15,000 = Rs. 2,25,000.\nOption (1), Rs. 2,22,000, wrongly applies the lower-of rule to stationery as well.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA) describes financial statements as reflecting a combination of three elements. Which option correctly identifies them?","image":"","options":["Recorded facts, accounting principles and personal judgements","Market values, accounting standards and auditors' opinions","Recorded facts, current replacement costs and management forecasts","Budgeted figures, accounting conventions and legal requirements"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"AICPA says financial statements present a periodical review of the management's progress. They deal with the status of investment and the results achieved.\nAICPA states that they reflect a combination of recorded facts, accounting principles and personal judgements.\nOption (2) fails because statements are based on historical cost, not market values.\nOptions (3) and (4) fail because forecasts and budgeted figures are not recorded facts.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Ritika Ltd. bought pens, pencils and postage stamps worth Rs 1,800 during the year. Although these are assets in nature, the whole amount was charged as an expense in the same year. Which accounting convention is being followed?","image":"","options":["Convention of conservatism","Convention of consistency","Convention of materiality","Convention of full disclosure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: under the convention of materiality, small items like pens, pencils and postage stamps are treated as expenditure in the year of purchase. This is done even though they are assets in nature.\nWhy (3) fits: the amount is too small to affect the users' decisions.\nWhy conservatism fails: conservatism is about avoiding overstatement of assets and income. It is not about how insignificant the amount is.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, what is the primary objective of the financial statements of a company?","image":"","options":["To calculate the income tax payable by the company","To assist users in their decision-making","To record transactions in chronological order","To show the current market value of all assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Financial statements give shareholders and other external parties information about the profitability and financial position of a company.\nNCERT states that their primary objective is to assist users in decision-making.\nTax calculation is only one use, and it is made by one group of users.\nChronological recording is done in the journal, not in financial statements.\nFinancial statements use historical cost, so they do not show current market values.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (items) with List-II (heading/sub-heading in the balance sheet):\nList-I\nA. Trade receivables realisable 15 months after the reporting date\nB. Software being developed in-house, not yet ready for use\nC. Stores and spare parts\nD. Balance with bank in current account\nList-II\n(i) Inventories\n(ii) Cash and cash equivalents\n(iii) Other non-current assets\n(iv) Fixed assets – Intangible assets under development","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Trade receivables realised beyond 12 months or the operating cycle go under 'Other non-current assets'.\nB-(iv): An intangible asset that is not yet ready is shown as 'Intangible assets under development'.\nC-(i): Stores and spares are inventories, which are always current.\nD-(ii): A bank balance is part of cash and cash equivalents, which are always current and disclosed as per AS-3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"On 31 March 2017, the books of Ritika Ltd. show the following balances: Securities Premium Rs. 30,000; General Reserve Rs. 1,00,000; Statement of Profit and Loss (Cr.) Rs. 2,00,000. Preliminary expenses of Rs. 50,000 were incurred during the year and are to be written off as per the guidance in NCERT. What amount will be shown as 'Reserves and Surplus' in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,80,000","Rs. 3,30,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 2,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: Preliminary expenses are written off fully in the year they are incurred. They are written off first from securities premium, and any balance is written off from the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nSecurities premium: 30,000 - 30,000 = Nil.\nRemaining preliminary expenses: 50,000 - 30,000 = 20,000, written off from P&L. P&L balance becomes 2,00,000 - 20,000 = 1,80,000.\nReserves and Surplus = 1,00,000 + 1,80,000 = Rs. 2,80,000.\nOption (2) ignores the write-off. Option (4) wrongly writes off the full Rs. 50,000 from P&L and also removes the premium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match the items in List-I with the sub-heads in List-II under which they appear in the balance sheet of a company (Schedule III):\nList-I\n(A) Public deposits\n(B) Loose tools\n(C) Goodwill\n(D) Unpaid dividend\nList-II\n(i) Intangible assets\n(ii) Other current liabilities\n(iii) Long-term borrowings\n(iv) Inventories","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Public deposits are normally accepted for periods beyond 12 months, so they go under Long-term borrowings: (iii).\n(B) Loose tools are shown under Inventories: (iv).\n(C) Goodwill has no physical form, so it is a fixed asset under Intangible assets: (i).\n(D) Unpaid dividend is a declared amount still payable, so it goes under Other current liabilities: (ii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Financial statements show shareholders how the management has performed. This helps them see the gap between management performance and what the owners expected. Which use of financial statements does this describe?","image":"","options":["Basis for fiscal policies","Report on stewardship function","Basis for granting of credit","Helps stock exchanges"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Managers run the company on behalf of the shareholders, who are its owners. Financial statements report the managers' performance back to the owners. This is called the stewardship function.\nIt lets owners see the gap between management performance and their own expectations.\nFiscal policy relates to government taxation, and credit granting relates to lenders. Neither is about reporting to owners.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Machine purchased in 2015 for Rs 10,00,000 is shown in the 2024-25 balance sheet of Arya Ltd. at Rs 10,00,000 less accumulated depreciation. Its replacement cost today is Rs 16,00,000.\nStatement I: Showing the machine at cost less depreciation instead of Rs 16,00,000 is consistent with the going concern postulate.\nStatement II: The money measurement postulate assumes the value of money stays the same over time, so the amount originally paid is kept even though purchasing power has changed.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: going concern assumes the business will continue for a long time. Assets are therefore shown on historical cost basis, and the balance sheet uses cost less depreciation. Replacement or market value is not used.\nStatement II is correct: money measurement assumes the value of money is unchanged. So assets bought at different times keep the amount paid for them, despite drastic changes in purchasing power.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are specific objectives of financial statements as stated in NCERT?\n(i) Providing information about economic resources and obligations of a business\n(ii) Providing information about the earning capacity of the business\n(iii) Guaranteeing the current market value of the company's assets\n(iv) Providing information about cash flows\n(v) Judging the effectiveness of management\n(vi) Reporting activities of the business affecting society\n(vii) Providing qualitative information about labour relations and quality of work\n(viii) Disclosing accounting policies","image":"","options":["Five","Seven","Six","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i), (ii), (iv), (v), (vi) and (viii) are the six specific objectives listed in NCERT.\n(iii) is not an objective: statements are based on historical cost and do not reflect current market values, which NCERT lists as a limitation.\n(vii) is not an objective: NCERT lists the absence of qualitative information, such as labour relations, as a limitation.\nThat gives six objectives.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Bills receivable arising from the sale of goods in the normal course of business, due within three months, are shown in the balance sheet of a company under:","image":"","options":["Cash and cash equivalents","Short-term loans and advances","Current investments","Trade receivables"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Under Schedule III, 'Sundry debtors' has been replaced by the term 'Trade receivables'. This head covers both debtors and bills receivable arising from sales.\nThese bills will be realised within 12 months, so they are current assets.\nThey are not loans and advances, because they arise from trading, not from lending.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding classification of liabilities as per Schedule III is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Current maturities of long-term loans are shown under 'Other current liabilities'.","Loans repayable on demand are classified as short-term borrowings.","Deferred tax liabilities (net) are always non-current.","Share application money pending allotment, to the extent non-refundable, is shown under current liabilities."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: amounts of long-term debt repayable within 12 months go to 'Other current liabilities'. \n(2) Correct: loans repayable on demand are short-term borrowings. \n(3) Correct: deferred tax liabilities and assets are always non-current. \n(4) Incorrect: such money, not exceeding the issued capital and to the extent non-refundable, is non-current. It is shown as a separate head, 'Share application money pending allotment', on the face of the balance sheet. \nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Nirmal Ltd. issued 6,000, 9% debentures of ₹100 each at par, redeemable at a premium of 10% after 6 years. The loss on issue was written off in the same year. At what amount will 'Premium on redemption of debentures' appear under 'Non-current liabilities – Other long-term liabilities'?","image":"","options":["₹6,00,000","₹60,000","₹6,60,000","₹54,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 6,000 debentures of ₹100 each, redeemable at a 10% premium after 6 years.\nFormula: Premium on redemption = Number of debentures × Face value × Rate of premium.\nSubstituting: 6,000 × ₹100 × 10% = ₹60,000.\nThe premium is payable only on redemption, which is more than 12 months away. So it is shown under Other long-term liabilities.\nThe debentures of ₹6,00,000 themselves go under Long-term borrowings.\nSo premium on redemption = ₹60,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are required to be disclosed in the 'Notes to Accounts' on share capital as per Schedule III?\n(i) Number of shares held by each shareholder holding more than 5% shares\n(ii) Par value per share\n(iii) Aggregate amount of calls unpaid\n(iv) Number and class of shares allotted as fully paid bonus shares during the 5 years immediately preceding the Balance Sheet date\n(v) Names and addresses of all shareholders holding less than 1% shares","image":"","options":["Five","Four","Three","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Required: it identifies the ultimate owners of the company.\n(ii) Required: par value per share must be stated for each class.\n(iii) Required: calls unpaid are disclosed in aggregate.\n(iv) Required: bonus shares issued in the preceding 5 years must be disclosed.\n(v) Not required: there is no such disclosure for small shareholders.\nFour of the five are required.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A citizens' group in a town near the factory of Green Valley Ltd. wants to know how the company's operations affect the local environment and community. The group plans to read the company's financial statements for this purpose.\nWhich objective of financial statements is the group relying on?","image":"","options":["To judge the effectiveness of management","To provide information about the earning capacity of the business","To report information about activities of the business affecting society","To provide information about economic resources and obligations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists reporting the activities of the business that affect society as one objective of financial statements.\nSuch activities should be ones that can be determined, described or measured and are important in the social environment.\nThe group is concerned with the company's impact on its community and environment, so this objective fits.\n'Effectiveness of management' is about using resources efficiently, not social impact.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of financial statements?\n(i) They are based on historical cost and do not reflect the current market situation.\n(ii) They show aggregate information rather than detailed information.\n(iii) They do not contain qualitative information such as labour relations.\n(iv) They form the basis for granting credit by banks.\n(v) They are only interim reports.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Limitation: assets are shown at historical cost.\n(ii) Limitation: only aggregate information is given.\n(iii) Limitation: there is no qualitative information, such as industrial relations or quality of work.\n(iv) Not a limitation: serving as a basis for granting credit is a use of financial statements.\n(v) Limitation: profit is for a specified period and the position shown is true only on that date.\nFour of the five are limitations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are required to be disclosed for the period of 5 years immediately preceding the balance sheet date?\n(i) Aggregate number and class of shares allotted as fully paid bonus shares\n(ii) Aggregate number and class of shares bought back\n(iii) Aggregate number and class of shares allotted as fully paid under contracts without payment received in cash\n(iv) Shares held by each shareholder holding more than 5% shares\n(v) Par value per share","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: bonus shares are a 5-year disclosure.\n(ii) Yes: shares bought back are a 5-year disclosure.\n(iii) Yes: shares allotted for consideration other than cash are a 5-year disclosure.\n(iv) No: holdings above 5% are disclosed as at the reporting date.\n(v) No: par value is disclosed for each class as a current detail.\nSo three items need the 5-year disclosure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"As per Schedule III of the Companies Act, 2013, the notes to accounts on share capital must show the shares held by each shareholder who holds more than a certain percentage of shares, with the number of shares held. What is that percentage?","image":"","options":["2%","5%","10%","25%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III aims to make clear who the ultimate owners of the company are.\nTo do this, it requires disclosure of the shares held by each shareholder holding more than 5% of the shares, with the number of shares specified.\n10% and 25% are not the Schedule III thresholds for this note.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Arushi Ltd. issued 5,000, 10% debentures of Rs. 100 each at par, redeemable at a premium of 5% after 5 years. How will the premium on redemption of debentures be shown in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 25,000 under Non-current Liabilities – Other long-term liabilities","Rs. 25,000 under Non-current Liabilities – Long-term borrowings","Rs. 5,25,000 under Non-current Liabilities – Long-term borrowings","Rs. 25,000 under Current Liabilities – Short-term provisions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Premium on redemption = 5,000 × Rs. 100 × 5% = Rs. 25,000.\nThis amount is payable only at redemption, which is after 5 years. So it is a non-current liability.\nNCERT shows it as Other long-term liabilities, with a Note to Accounts.\nThe debentures themselves (Rs. 5,00,000) appear separately under Long-term borrowings. The premium is not added to them, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Mr. Rao has held 2,000 equity shares of Sunrise Ltd. for six years. After reading the latest balance sheet and statement of profit and loss, he wants to judge the status, safety and return on his holding. He will then decide whether to keep the shares or sell them. Which use of financial statements best matches Mr. Rao's purpose?","image":"","options":["Basis for prospective investors","Guide to the value of the investment already made","Report on stewardship function","Basis for granting of credit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mr. Rao already owns the shares, so he is an existing shareholder, not a prospective investor. This rules out option (1).\nNCERT says existing shareholders want to know the status, safety and return on their investment. They also use this to decide whether to continue or discontinue it. This is 'guide to the value of the investment already made'.\nStewardship is about judging management's performance against owners' expectations, not about the hold-or-sell decision. This rules out option (3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Vista Ltd. gives the following balances as at 31 March 2017:\nSecurities Premium Rs. 30,000\nPreliminary expenses incurred during the year Rs. 1,00,000\nGeneral Reserve Rs. 1,00,000\nStatement of Profit and Loss (Cr.) Rs. 2,50,000\nMoney received against share warrants Rs. 50,000\nWhat amount will be shown against 'Reserves and Surplus' on the face of the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,30,000","Rs. 3,80,000","Rs. 2,80,000","Rs. 2,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: Write off the preliminary expenses first from securities premium: 30,000 − 1,00,000 = (70,000).\nStep 2: Add the remaining items of Reserves and Surplus: (70,000) + 1,00,000 (General Reserve) + 2,50,000 (Statement of Profit and Loss) = 2,80,000.\nStep 3: Money received against share warrants is a separate line item under Shareholders' Funds, so it is not included.\nAdding the warrant money gives Rs. 3,30,000, and ignoring the preliminary expenses gives Rs. 3,80,000. Both are wrong.\nSo Reserves and Surplus = Rs. 2,80,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Every company registered under the Companies Act, 2013 must prepare its balance sheet, statement of profit and loss and notes to accounts in the manner prescribed in which part of the Act?","image":"","options":["Schedule I","Schedule II","Schedule III","Schedule IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that every company registered under the Companies Act, 2013 must prepare its balance sheet, statement of profit and loss and notes to accounts as per the revised Schedule III.\nThis harmonises disclosure requirements with the accounting standards.\nSchedule III also prescribes the vertical format for these statements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Under the revised Schedule III, amounts due from customers for goods sold in the normal course of business, earlier called 'Sundry Debtors', are now shown as:","image":"","options":["Trade Payables","Short-term loans and advances","Trade Receivables","Other current liabilities"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schedule III replaced the term 'Sundry Debtors' with 'Trade Receivables'.\nIt replaced 'Sundry Creditors' with 'Trade Payables'.\nAmounts due from customers are assets, so they are Trade Receivables.\nTrade Payables is the tempting distractor, but it is the new name for creditors, which is a liability.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Tanvi Ltd. has a debit balance of Rs. 4,00,000 in its statement of profit and loss for the current year. As per Schedule III, how should this balance be presented in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Under 'Other non-current assets' on the assets side","As a negative figure under 'Surplus' in 'Reserves and Surplus'","As a deduction from 'Share Capital'","Under 'Other current assets'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III requires a debit balance of the statement of profit and loss to be disclosed as a negative figure under the head 'Surplus'.\nThis head falls within Reserves and Surplus under Shareholders' Funds.\nThe total of Reserves and Surplus is shown even if it turns negative.\nShowing the loss as an asset (options 1 and 4) was the old practice and is not allowed now.\nIt is also not deducted from share capital.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Financial statements should disclose the significant accounting policies followed and any changes made in them during the year.\nReason (R): Knowing the policies and concepts followed in accounting helps users understand the financial statements better.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: disclosing accounting policies is one of the specific objectives of financial statements listed in NCERT.\nThis disclosure covers the significant policies and concepts followed, and changes made in them during the year.\nR is true: NCERT says this disclosure is needed so that users can understand the statements in a better way.\nR therefore states the purpose of A, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (items in the books of a company) with List-II (head or sub-head under Schedule III):\nList-I\nA. Cash credit from bank repayable on demand\nB. Provision for tax to be settled within 12 months\nC. Term loan from a financial institution repayable after 5 years\nD. Liability arising from timing differences in tax\nList-II\nI. Long-term borrowings\nII. Deferred tax liabilities (net)\nIII. Short-term borrowings\nIV. Short-term provisions","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-III: Loans repayable on demand are short-term borrowings.\nB-IV: A provision settled within 12 months is a short-term provision under current liabilities.\nC-I: A loan repayable after more than 12 months is a long-term borrowing.\nD-II: Deferred tax liability is always non-current and has its own line item.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the case of a finance company, interest and dividend earned form part of 'Revenue from operations'.\nStatement II: In the case of a company other than a finance company, dividend income is also shown under 'Revenue from operations'.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: for a finance company, revenue from operations includes interest, dividend and income from other financial services.\nStatement II is incorrect: for other companies, dividend income is non-operating and is shown under 'Other income'.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Depreciation on plant and machinery and the writing off of goodwill are both shown under the same head in the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nReason (R): Amortisation refers to the diminution in the value of tangible fixed assets.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Schedule III has a single head called 'Depreciation and amortisation expense'. Depreciation on plant and the amortisation of goodwill both appear under it.\nR is false. Depreciation is the diminution in the value of fixed assets. Amortisation is writing off the amount of intangible assets such as goodwill and patents.\nSo R wrongly links amortisation to tangible assets.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the Notes to Accounts on share capital, calls in arrears are deducted from called-up capital under the heading 'Subscribed but not fully paid-up capital'.\nReason (R): Calls in arrears represent the amount called up by the company but not yet received from shareholders.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In Dinkar Ltd, 300 shares fully called up are worth ₹30,000. Calls in arrears of ₹6,000 are deducted, giving ₹24,000 under 'subscribed but not fully paid'.\nR is true. Calls in arrears are the amounts called up but not paid by shareholders.\nThis unpaid amount is not actually paid-up capital, which is why it is deducted. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the presentation of financial statements under Schedule III of the Companies Act, 2013 is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["If there is a conflict, accounting standards prevail over Schedule III","Schedule III applies to insurance and banking companies as well","Disclosures on the face of the financial statements or in the notes are essential and mandatory","Assets and liabilities must be classified as current and non-current"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that accounting standards prevail over Schedule III.\n(2) Incorrect: Schedule III does not apply to insurance or banking companies.\nIt also does not apply to companies whose formats are specified under any other Act.\n(3) Correct: disclosures on the face of the statements or in the notes are mandatory.\n(4) Correct: Schedule III requires current and non-current classification of assets and liabilities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The following balances appear in the books of Tara Ltd. as at 31 March 2025:\nSecurities premium ₹30,000; Preliminary expenses (incurred during the year) ₹50,000; General reserve ₹1,00,000; Statement of profit and loss (Cr.) ₹2,00,000.\nWhat amount will be shown as 'Reserves and Surplus' in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["₹3,30,000","₹2,30,000","₹2,80,000","₹3,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rule: preliminary expenses must be written off fully in the year they are incurred. They are written off first from securities premium, and any balance from the statement of profit and loss.\nWorking: Securities premium ₹30,000 is used up fully. The balance ₹20,000 comes from the profit and loss balance.\nSurplus left = 2,00,000 − 20,000 = ₹1,80,000.\nReserves and Surplus = 0 + 1,00,000 + 1,80,000 = ₹2,80,000.\nThe same result: 30,000 − 50,000 + 1,00,000 + 2,00,000 = ₹2,80,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Objective of financial statements) with List-II (What it provides):\nList-I\n(A) Information about earning capacity\n(B) Judging effectiveness of management\n(C) Disclosing accounting policies\n(D) Information about economic resources and obligations\nList-II\n(i) Significant policies and concepts followed, and changes made in them during the year\n(ii) Data to predict, compare and evaluate the firm's earning capacity\n(iii) Adequate, reliable, periodical information to outsiders who have limited ability to obtain it\n(iv) Information on management's ability to use the business's resources effectively","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Earning capacity matches (ii): statements help predict, compare and evaluate earning capacity.\n(B) Effectiveness of management matches (iv): statements show how well management uses resources.\n(C) Accounting policies matches (i): statements disclose significant policies and changes made during the year.\n(D) Economic resources and obligations matches (iii): statements give reliable periodical information to outsiders with limited access.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Four parties plan to rely only on a company's published financial statements. Whose information need is the statements LEAST able to satisfy?","image":"","options":["A bank checking the company's capacity to repay a loan","An employees' union wanting to assess labour relations and quality of work life","An investor assessing the short-term and long-term solvency of the company","The government studying corporate profits while framing tax policy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Banks, investors and the government all need monetary data. Financial statements provide exactly this: the basis for credit, solvency and profitability analysis, and fiscal policy.\nThe union needs qualitative information, such as labour relations, industrial climate and quality of work.\nFinancial statements contain only monetary information. NCERT lists this as a limitation, so the union's need is least served.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company is building a new factory shed that is still under construction on the balance sheet date. The amount spent on it, ₹8,00,000, will be shown in the balance sheet under:","image":"","options":["Current assets – Other current assets","Non-current assets – Fixed assets – Intangible assets under development","Non-current assets – Fixed assets – Capital work-in-progress","Non-current assets – Non-current investments"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Schedule III divides fixed assets into four parts: tangible assets, intangible assets, capital work-in-progress and intangible assets under development.\nA factory shed still under construction is a tangible asset that is not yet complete. It is therefore shown as capital work-in-progress under Non-current assets → Fixed assets.\nWhy option (2) fails: 'Intangible assets under development' covers non-physical items being developed, such as software or patents. A shed is a physical asset.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The Board of Directors of Kaveri Ltd. proposed a final dividend of Rs. 3,00,000 for the year 2016–17 on 20th May 2017. The shareholders approved it at the AGM held on 25th September 2017. Which treatment is correct?","image":"","options":["Shown as a short-term provision under Current Liabilities in the balance sheet as at 31st March 2017","Disclosed as a contingent liability in Notes to Accounts as at 31st March 2017, and accounted as a liability in 2017–18 after approval","Deducted from Surplus in the balance sheet as at 31st March 2017, since the Board's proposal is final","Shown under Other current liabilities as at 31st March 2017, and the shareholders may increase the amount at the AGM"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The proposed dividend becomes a liability only after shareholders declare it at the AGM, which is held in the next financial year.\nUntil then it is contingent, so AS-4 requires it to be shown in Notes to Accounts as at 31-3-2017.\nOnce approved in September 2017, it is accounted as a liability during 2017–18.\nOption (4) also fails because shareholders may reduce the proposed dividend but cannot increase it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Proposed dividend for the current year is shown in the Notes to Accounts as a contingent liability and not as a liability on the face of the Balance Sheet.\nReason (R): Proposed dividend becomes payable only after the shareholders approve it in the Annual General Meeting, which is held in the next financial year.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. As per AS-4, proposed dividend is disclosed in the Notes to Accounts as a contingent liability.\nR is true. The Board of Directors only proposes the dividend. It is declared by the shareholders at the AGM, which is held after the year ends.\nR explains A. Because the dividend depends on shareholders' approval, it is not yet a liability on the Balance Sheet date.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the presentation of a company's balance sheet under Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013 are correct?\n(i) A horizontal (T-shaped) format of the balance sheet is prescribed.\n(ii) A debit balance of the Statement of Profit and Loss is shown as a negative figure under the head 'Surplus'.\n(iii) The terms 'Trade Receivables' and 'Trade Payables' are used in place of 'Sundry Debtors' and 'Sundry Creditors'.\n(iv) Where Schedule III and an accounting standard conflict, Schedule III prevails.\n(v) Disclosure of share application money pending allotment is mandatory.\n(vi) Insurance companies must also follow this format.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: Schedule III prescribes a vertical format.\n(ii) Correct: a debit balance of the Statement of Profit and Loss appears as a negative figure under 'Surplus'.\n(iii) Correct: 'Sundry Debtors' and 'Sundry Creditors' are replaced by 'Trade Receivables' and 'Trade Payables'.\n(iv) Incorrect: accounting standards prevail over Schedule III.\n(v) Correct: share application money pending allotment must be disclosed.\n(vi) Incorrect: Schedule III does not apply to insurance and banking companies.\nCorrect statements are (ii), (iii) and (v), so the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Users) with List-II (Use of financial statements):\nList-I\nA. Stock brokers\nB. Banks\nC. Existing shareholders\nD. Trade associations\nList-II\n(i) Developing standard ratios\n(ii) Deciding whether to continue holding the investment\n(iii) Deciding the prices to be quoted\n(iv) Sanctioning loans","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Stock brokers judge the financial position of companies and decide the prices to be quoted.\nB-(iv): Banks base their credit decisions on the company's financial performance.\nC-(ii): Existing shareholders decide whether to continue or discontinue their investment.\nD-(i): Trade associations develop standard ratios.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Under the current/non-current classification of Schedule III, which of the following items is ALWAYS treated as a current asset, whatever its expected period of realisation?","image":"","options":["Trade receivables","Investments","Inventories","Long-term loans and advances"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Schedule III treats all inventories as current, with no exception. \nTrade receivables are current only if realised within 12 months or the operating cycle. Beyond that, they go to 'Other non-current assets'. \nInvestments are split: current if expected to be realised within 12 months, otherwise non-current. \nLong-term loans and advances are non-current by their nature. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meghna Ltd. provides the following information as at 31 March 2025:\nEquity share capital ₹20,00,000; 10% Debentures ₹8,00,000; Public deposits ₹3,00,000; Bills payable ₹1,20,000; Creditors ₹1,80,000; Provision for taxation ₹1,00,000; Calls in advance ₹50,000; Proposed dividend for the year ₹2,00,000; Claims against the company not acknowledged as debt ₹70,000.\nWhat is the total of 'Current Liabilities' in its balance sheet?","image":"","options":["₹4,00,000","₹4,50,000","₹6,50,000","₹7,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Trade payables = Bills payable 1,20,000 + Creditors 1,80,000 = ₹3,00,000.\nOther current liabilities = Calls in advance ₹50,000.\nShort-term provisions = Provision for taxation ₹1,00,000.\nExcluded items:\n• Debentures and public deposits are Long-term borrowings.\n• Proposed dividend and claims not acknowledged as debt are contingent liabilities, shown only in Notes.\nTotal current liabilities = 3,00,000 + 50,000 + 1,00,000 = ₹4,50,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the limitations of financial statements are correct?\n(i) Results may show bias because they depend partly on personal judgements, such as estimates of the useful life of assets and provisions for doubtful debts.\n(ii) Financial statements give detailed information on every transaction, so they always fully support users' decisions.\n(iii) The financial position shown by the balance sheet is true only at that point of time, and likely changes on a future date are not shown.\n(iv) Because assets are recorded at historical cost, financial statements fully adjust for changes in the purchasing power of money.\n(v) Financial statements contain only monetary information.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: personal judgements and estimates can make the results biased.\n(ii) Incorrect: financial statements give aggregate, not detailed, information, so they may not help much in decision-making.\n(iii) Correct: this is the 'interim reports' limitation.\n(iv) Incorrect: historical cost ignores changes in purchasing power, so values do not reflect current market conditions.\n(v) Correct: qualitative information is excluded.\nCorrect statements = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Assets are shown on a going-concern basis at historical cost, so the balance sheet may overstate what the assets would fetch if the company were forced into liquidation.\nStatement II: Following the convention of conservatism completely removes bias from financial statements, because it rules out personal judgement.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. The going concern assumption leads to assets being shown at historical cost, and NCERT notes that such assets may not realise their stated values in a forced liquidation.\nStatement II is false. Conservatism itself is applied through personal opinion, estimates and judgements, for example in valuing inventory or creating provisions. So it cannot rule out judgement.\nNCERT lists bias as a limitation precisely because of these personal judgements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Ratan Ltd. has an annual turnover of Rs. 80 crore. Kiran Ltd. has an annual turnover of Rs. 250 crore. Both prepare their financial statements as per Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013.\nWhich of the following choices of rounding off is permissible for both companies?","image":"","options":["Ratan Ltd.: nearest thousands; Kiran Ltd.: nearest millions","Ratan Ltd.: nearest hundreds; Kiran Ltd.: nearest thousands","Ratan Ltd.: nearest lakhs; Kiran Ltd.: nearest hundreds","Ratan Ltd.: nearest thousands; Kiran Ltd.: nearest thousands"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rule: if turnover is below Rs. 100 crore, figures may be rounded to the nearest hundreds, thousands, lakhs or millions, or decimals thereof.\nIf turnover is above Rs. 100 crore, figures may be rounded only to the nearest lakhs or millions, or decimals thereof.\nRatan Ltd. (Rs. 80 crore) may use hundreds, thousands, lakhs or millions.\nKiran Ltd. (Rs. 250 crore) may use only lakhs or millions.\nOptions (2), (3) and (4) round Kiran Ltd. to hundreds or thousands, which is not allowed.\nOption (1) is valid for both companies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: On the face of the balance sheet, only the total of Share Capital appears under Shareholders' Funds; the details are given in the Notes to Accounts.\nStatement II: The reconciliation of the number of shares outstanding at the beginning and end of the reporting period is required only for equity shares, not for preference shares.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Only broad items are shown on the face of the balance sheet, and the details go into Notes to Accounts. For example, Dinkar Ltd shows ₹35,90,000 with a note reference.\nStatement II is false. The reconciliation of shares outstanding is required for each class of shares, preference shares included.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Preliminary expenses are written off completely in the year in which they are incurred, first from Securities Premium and then, for any balance, from the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nStatement II: Under Schedule III, unwritten-off preliminary expenses may be carried forward and shown under Reserves and Surplus as a positive item until fully written off.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that preliminary expenses are fully written off in the year they are incurred, first from securities premium and the balance from the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nStatement II is incorrect. Because the expenses are written off in the same year, nothing is carried forward.\nPreliminary expenses also reduce reserves, so they could never appear as a positive item under Reserves and Surplus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about presentation of financial statements under Schedule III are correct?\n(i) A vertical format of presentation is prescribed.\n(ii) Rounding off of figures is optional and left to the company's discretion.\n(iii) Disclosure of share application money pending allotment is mandatory.\n(iv) Schedule III overrides Accounting Standards in case of conflict.\n(v) Assets and liabilities are to be bifurcated into current and non-current.\n(vi) A debit balance of the statement of profit and loss is shown on the assets side.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a vertical format is prescribed.\n(ii) Incorrect: rounding off is mandatory and depends on turnover.\n(iii) Correct: disclosure of share application money pending allotment is mandatory.\n(iv) Incorrect: Accounting Standards prevail over Schedule III.\n(v) Correct: current/non-current bifurcation applies.\n(vi) Incorrect: a debit balance is shown as a negative figure under 'Surplus' in Reserves and Surplus.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"In the Statement of Profit and Loss prepared as per Schedule III, Earnings per equity share is disclosed in which two forms?","image":"","options":["Gross and Net","Basic and Diluted","Operating and Non-operating","Current and Deferred"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Item XVI of the Schedule III format is 'Earnings per equity share', with two parts: (1) Basic and (2) Diluted.\n'Current and Deferred' are the two parts of tax expense (item X), so that option is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (items) with List-II (sub-heads under Current Liabilities):\nList-I:\nA. Bank overdraft\nB. Bills payable\nC. Provision for taxation\nD. Interest accrued but not due on debentures\nList-II:\n(i) Trade payables\n(ii) Short-term provisions\n(iii) Short-term borrowings\n(iv) Other current liabilities","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): a bank overdraft is repayable on demand, so it is a short-term borrowing.\nB-(i): bills payable (acceptances) arise from purchases in the normal course of business, so they are trade payables.\nC-(ii): provision for taxation settled within 12 months is a short-term provision.\nD-(iv): interest accrued on debentures but not yet due is an Other current liability.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding the classification of liabilities as per Schedule III is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Loans repayable on demand are shown as short-term borrowings.","The instalment of a long-term loan repayable within the next twelve months is shown under other current liabilities.","Deferred tax liabilities (net) are always classified as non-current.","Trade payables to be settled beyond 12 months from the Balance Sheet date are shown under short-term borrowings."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: loans repayable on demand, or with an original tenure of 12 months or less, are short-term borrowings.\n(2) Correct: current maturities of long-term debt go under other current liabilities, with a note.\n(3) Correct: deferred tax assets and liabilities are always non-current.\n(4) Incorrect: trade payables settled beyond 12 months or beyond the operating cycle are shown under 'Other long-term liabilities'.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements on the rounding-off rule under Schedule III:\nStatement I: Kiran Ltd., with a turnover of Rs. 80 crore, may present the figures in its financial statements rounded off to the nearest thousands.\nStatement II: Vayu Ltd., with a turnover of Rs. 150 crore, may present the figures in its financial statements rounded off to the nearest thousands.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The rounding-off rule depends on turnover.\nIf turnover is below Rs. 100 crore, figures may be rounded to the nearest hundreds, thousands, lakhs or millions, or decimals thereof.\nIf turnover is above Rs. 100 crore, figures may be rounded only to the nearest lakhs or millions, or decimals thereof.\nStatement I: Rs. 80 crore is below Rs. 100 crore, so rounding to thousands is allowed. Correct.\nStatement II: Rs. 150 crore is above Rs. 100 crore, so rounding to thousands is not allowed. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Meera Traders Ltd. for the year ended 31st March, 2017, calculate the Profit before tax as per Schedule III:\nRevenue from operations Rs. 12,00,000; Interest on fixed deposits Rs. 20,000; Purchases of stock-in-trade Rs. 5,00,000; Opening stock-in-trade Rs. 60,000; Closing stock-in-trade Rs. 90,000; Wages Rs. 1,00,000; Salaries Rs. 60,000; 12% Debentures Rs. 2,00,000 (issued on 1st April, 2016); Bank charges Rs. 4,000; Depreciation Rs. 30,000; Rent paid Rs. 16,000.","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,16,000","Rs. 4,56,000","Rs. 4,96,000","Rs. 5,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total revenue = 12,00,000 + 20,000 (other income) = Rs. 12,20,000.\nExpenses: purchases 5,00,000; change in inventories (60,000 − 90,000) = (30,000); employee benefits 1,60,000; finance cost 12% × 2,00,000 = 24,000; depreciation 30,000; other expenses (4,000 + 16,000) = 20,000.\nSo total expenses = Rs. 7,04,000.\nPBT = 12,20,000 − 7,04,000 = Rs. 5,16,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Neha Ltd. has the following balances: Securities Premium Rs. 50,000; Preliminary expenses incurred during the year Rs. 80,000; Statement of Profit and Loss (Cr.) Rs. 2,00,000. If preliminary expenses are written off as per NCERT treatment, what is the total of 'Reserves and Surplus'?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 1,70,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 3,30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Securities Premium = Rs. 50,000; preliminary expenses = Rs. 80,000; Statement of Profit and Loss (Cr.) = Rs. 2,00,000.\nRule: preliminary expenses are written off in full in the year they are incurred. They are written off first from securities premium, and any balance from the Statement of Profit and Loss.\nSecurities premium after write-off: 50,000 − 80,000 = (30,000).\nTotal Reserves and Surplus: (30,000) + 2,00,000 = 1,70,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Financial statements are prepared from the summarised results of the accounting process. Which description of financial statements is given in NCERT?","image":"","options":["They are the starting point of the accounting process","They are the end products of the accounting process","They are subsidiary books of original entry","They are internal working papers prepared only for management"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT calls financial statements the end products of the accounting process. They come out of the summarising stage of accounting.\nWhy (2) fits: transactions are recorded and summarised first, and the statements are prepared last.\nWhy the others fail: books of original entry are the starting point, not the end. Financial statements are also formal annual reports for owners and external parties, not only for management.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the uses of financial statements are correct?\n(i) Stock exchanges use them to judge how transparent a company's reporting is and to call for information that protects investors.\n(ii) Credit-granting institutions take lending decisions based on the financial performance they reveal.\n(iii) They provide qualitative information such as labour relations and quality of work, which is useful to employees.\n(iv) They provide basic input for the industrial and taxation policies of the government.\n(v) Shareholders cannot use them to decide whether to continue or discontinue their investment.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is how financial statements help stock exchanges.\n(ii) Correct: they form the basis for granting credit.\n(iii) Incorrect: they contain only monetary information. The absence of qualitative information is one of their limitations.\n(iv) Correct: they are a basis for fiscal policies.\n(v) Incorrect: they are a guide to the value of investment already made, including the decision to continue or discontinue.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are shown under 'Reserves and Surplus' in a company's balance sheet as per Schedule III?\n(i) Capital Redemption Reserve\n(ii) Securities Premium Reserve\n(iii) Share Options Outstanding Account\n(iv) Calls-in-arrears\n(v) Revaluation Reserve\n(vi) Share Forfeiture Account","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Included: Capital Redemption Reserve is listed under Reserves and Surplus.\n(ii) Included: Securities Premium Reserve is listed under Reserves and Surplus.\n(iii) Included: Share Options Outstanding Account is recognised as a separate item under Reserves and Surplus.\n(iv) Not included: calls-in-arrears are deducted from subscribed capital in the Share Capital note.\n(v) Included: Revaluation Reserve is listed under Reserves and Surplus.\n(vi) Not included: forfeited shares are added to Share Capital (see Illustration 1).\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The following balances appear in the books of Meher Ltd. on 31st March 2025:\nStock-in-trade Rs. 1,40,000; Loose tools Rs. 12,000; Bills receivable Rs. 1,20,000; Trade receivables realisable after 18 months Rs. 50,000; Cash at bank Rs. 1,35,000; Investments to be sold within 3 months Rs. 60,000; Long-term investment in shares Rs. 2,00,000; Capital work-in-progress Rs. 80,000.\nWhat is the total of Current assets in its balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,55,000","Rs. 4,67,000","Rs. 5,17,000","Rs. 6,67,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Inventories: 1,40,000 + 12,000 = Rs. 1,52,000\nTrade receivables (bills receivable): Rs. 1,20,000\nCash and cash equivalents: Rs. 1,35,000\nCurrent investments: Rs. 60,000\nTotal current assets: 1,52,000 + 1,20,000 + 1,35,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 4,67,000\nExcluded as non-current: receivables due after 18 months (other non-current assets), the long-term investment, and capital work-in-progress.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following companies is NOT required to prepare its balance sheet in the format prescribed by Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013?","image":"","options":["A company manufacturing cement","A banking company","A company trading in electronic goods","A company providing software services"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III applies to all Indian companies that prepare financial statements under the Companies Act, 2013.\nIt does not apply to insurance or banking companies.\nIt also does not apply to a company whose form of balance sheet is laid down by another Act.\nManufacturing, trading and service companies have no separate prescribed format, so they must follow Schedule III.\nA banking company prepares its accounts in the form prescribed by its own governing law.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match the major heads in List-I with the sub-heads in List-II as per the balance sheet format of Schedule III:\nList-I\n(A) Shareholders' Funds\n(B) Non-current Liabilities\n(C) Current Liabilities\n(D) Current Assets\nList-II\n(i) Trade payables\n(ii) Cash and cash equivalents\n(iii) Money received against share warrants\n(iv) Long-term provisions","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Shareholders' Funds has three sub-heads: share capital, reserves and surplus, and money received against share warrants. So A matches (iii).\n(B) Long-term provisions are shown under Non-current Liabilities. So B matches (iv).\n(C) Trade payables are shown under Current Liabilities. So C matches (i).\n(D) Cash and cash equivalents are always current and appear under Current Assets. So D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"According to the American Institute of Certified Public Accountants (AICPA), financial statements reflect a combination of:","image":"","options":["Recorded facts, accounting principles and personal judgements","Market values, tax rules and management forecasts","Recorded facts, budgets and auditor's opinions","Current costs, replacement values and accounting standards"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: the AICPA says financial statements present a periodical review of progress by management.\nIt adds that they reflect a combination of recorded facts, accounting principles and personal judgements.\nWhy the others fail: market values, replacement values and forecasts are not the basis. Statements use historical cost and recorded data.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Trade payables of a company that are to be settled 18 months after the balance sheet date are shown under 'Other long-term liabilities'.\nReason (R): Under Schedule III, trade payables are always classified as current liabilities, whatever their settlement period.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Trade payables to be settled beyond 12 months or beyond the operating cycle are shown under 'Other long-term liabilities', with a Note to Accounts.\nR is false. Trade payables are split into current and non-current portions. Only the balance payable within 12 months or the operating cycle appears under Current Liabilities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company is building a new factory shed that was still under construction on the balance sheet date. Under which head and sub-head will the amount spent on it appear?","image":"","options":["Current assets – Other current assets","Non-current assets – Non-current investments","Non-current assets – Fixed assets – Capital work-in-progress","Non-current assets – Long-term loans and advances"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fixed assets have four sub-heads: tangible assets, intangible assets, capital work-in-progress, and intangible assets under development.\nAn asset still under construction is shown as capital work-in-progress.\nIt is not an investment, because the company is building it for its own use.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Prospective investors study the financial statements of a company before buying its shares.\nReason (R): Investors mainly look for security and liquidity of their investment with reasonable profitability, and financial statements help them assess short-term and long-term solvency as well as profitability.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Financial statements are a basis for decisions by prospective investors.\nR is true. NCERT names security and liquidity, with reasonable profitability, as investors' prime considerations. It also says financial statements reveal solvency and profitability.\nR explains A: investors read the statements precisely because they show these qualities.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following figures in financial statements depend on personal opinion, estimates or judgement, as explained in NCERT?\n(i) Depreciation charged on the basis of the estimated useful economic life of machinery\n(ii) Provision for doubtful debts\n(iii) Deciding the cost and the market value of inventory when valuing it at the lower of the two\n(iv) Cash in hand taken as per the cash book\n(v) Bank balance taken as per the bank column of the cash book","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Judgement: depreciation depends on the estimated useful economic life of the asset.\n(ii) Judgement: provision for doubtful debts is based on estimates.\n(iii) Judgement: deciding the cost or market value of inventory needs several judgements.\n(iv) and (v) Recorded facts: cash in hand and bank balance are taken directly from the books.\nSo 3 items involve judgement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Vikas Ltd. purchased land for ₹ 30,00,000 in April 2008. By 31st March, 2017 its market value had risen to ₹ 75,00,000. The land has not been revalued, and no depreciation is charged on land. At what amount will the land appear in the balance sheet as at 31st March, 2017?","image":"","options":["₹ 30,00,000","₹ 75,00,000","₹ 45,00,000","₹ 52,50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: cost of land = ₹ 30,00,000; market value = ₹ 75,00,000; no revaluation; no depreciation on land.\nRule: assets are recorded and shown at original (historical) cost, not at market price.\nSo the land appears at its cost of ₹ 30,00,000.\nThis amount is ₹ 45,00,000 below the current market value. It shows the limitation that financial statements do not reflect the current situation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Under Schedule III, a company discloses in its Notes to Accounts the aggregate number and class of shares allotted as fully paid bonus shares, shares bought back and shares allotted for consideration other than cash. These disclosures cover the period of how many years immediately preceding the balance sheet date?","image":"","options":["3 years","5 years","7 years","10 years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Schedule III requires three items to be disclosed for the 5 years immediately preceding the balance sheet date:\n(a) shares allotted as fully paid without receiving cash\n(b) bonus shares allotted\n(c) shares bought back\nThe other periods do not match the prescribed requirement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"The accountant of Meghna Ltd., a manufacturing company with a turnover of Rs. 60 crore, prepared the balance sheet as follows:\n(i) Presented it in the T-shaped (horizontal) form with liabilities on the left and assets on the right.\n(ii) Labelled amounts owed to suppliers of goods as 'Sundry Creditors'.\n(iii) Rounded off all figures to the nearest thousands.\n(iv) Showed share application money pending allotment as a separate item on the face of the balance sheet.\nWhich of these practices comply with Schedule III?","image":"","options":["(i) and (ii) only","(iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Does not comply: Schedule III prescribes the vertical format, not the T-form.\n(ii) Does not comply: 'Sundry Creditors' has been replaced by 'Trade Payables'.\n(iii) Complies: turnover is below Rs. 100 crore, so rounding to the nearest thousands is permitted.\n(iv) Complies: disclosure of share application money pending allotment on the face of the balance sheet is mandatory.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Zenith Ltd. has taken a loan from its bank that is repayable on demand. Under which sub-head will this loan appear in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Long-term borrowings","Other long-term liabilities","Short-term borrowings","Trade payables"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Loans repayable on demand are classified as short-term borrowings.\nThe same applies to loans whose original tenure is not more than twelve months or the operating cycle.\nShort-term borrowings are shown under Current Liabilities.\nLong-term borrowings cover only loans repayable after more than twelve months or the operating cycle. A demand loan can be recalled at any time, so it cannot be long-term.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Sparsh Ltd. bought pens, pencils and postage stamps worth Rs. 3,500 during the year. Some of these items remained unused at the year-end. The accountant still charged the full Rs. 3,500 as an expense of the year instead of showing the unused portion as an asset. Which accounting convention justifies this treatment?","image":"","options":["Convention of conservatism","Convention of materiality","Convention of consistency","Convention of full disclosure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that small items like pencils, pens and postage stamps are treated as expenditure in the year of purchase, even though they are assets in nature.\nThis follows the convention of materiality. Insignificant amounts need not be tracked as assets.\nConservatism is the tempting option, but it concerns avoiding overstatement through prudent estimates. It does not concern the size of an item.\nConsistency and full disclosure are unrelated to this treatment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Interest received on fixed deposits by a company that is NOT a finance company is shown in the Statement of Profit and Loss under:","image":"","options":["Revenue from operations","Other income","Finance costs (as a deduction)","Exceptional items"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Interest income is listed under 'Other income' for a company other than a finance company.\nFor a finance company, interest is part of revenue from operations because earning interest is its main business.\nFinance costs cover interest paid on borrowings, not interest received.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Zenith Ltd. received money from investors against instruments that will be converted into equity shares at a specified rate on a specified date. Under which head is this amount shown in the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["As a separate line item under Shareholders' Funds, named 'Money received against share warrants'","Under Reserves and Surplus as Securities Premium","Under Non-current Liabilities as Long-term borrowings","Under 'Share application money pending allotment'"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Money received in this way, for later conversion into shares at a specified rate and date, is money received against share warrants.\nSchedule III requires it to be shown as a separate line item, (c), under Shareholders' Funds, after Share Capital and Reserves and Surplus.\nIt is not share application money pending allotment. That is a separate head (2) in the balance sheet format.\nIt is not a borrowing either, because the money will become share capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The following balances are taken from the books of Meghna Ltd. as at 31st March, 2024:\nStock-in-trade ₹1,40,000; Loose tools ₹12,000; Stores and spares ₹30,000; Bills receivable ₹1,20,000; Debtors (realisable within 3 months) ₹80,000; Debtors (realisable after 18 months) ₹40,000; Cash at bank ₹1,35,000; Cash in hand ₹15,000; Investments to be sold within 6 months ₹50,000; Investments held for 3 years ₹2,00,000; Capital work-in-progress ₹1,00,000; Patents ₹60,000.\nWhat is the total of 'Current Assets' shown in the balance sheet as per Schedule III?","image":"","options":["₹6,22,000","₹5,82,000","₹7,82,000","₹5,32,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the classification rules of Schedule III.\nInventories (always current) = 1,40,000 + 12,000 + 30,000 = ₹1,82,000.\nTrade receivables (current) = 1,20,000 + 80,000 = ₹2,00,000. Debtors realisable after 18 months (₹40,000) go to Other non-current assets.\nCash and cash equivalents = 1,35,000 + 15,000 = ₹1,50,000.\nCurrent investments = ₹50,000. Investments held for 3 years, capital work-in-progress and patents are non-current.\nTotal current assets = 1,82,000 + 2,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 50,000 = ₹5,82,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Financial statements do not show the current financial condition of a business.\nReason (R): Assets bought at different times and at different prices are put together and shown at their historical cost rather than at market prices.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that financial statements do not show the current financial condition of the concern.\nR is true: transactions are recorded at original or historical cost. Assets bought at different times are added together at those costs, not at market prices.\nR explains A: the figures are based on past costs instead of present market values. That is exactly why the statements cannot show the current condition.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"As per Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013, under which head does a company show 'Deferred tax liabilities (net)' in its balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Current liabilities, if they are expected to reverse within 12 months","Non-current liabilities, always","Short-term provisions","Reserves and Surplus under Shareholders' Funds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Under Schedule III, deferred tax assets and liabilities are always non-current.\nIn the vertical format, 'Deferred tax liabilities (net)' is item (b) under Non-current Liabilities.\nOption (1) is tempting because most items use the 12-month rule. That rule does not apply to deferred tax.\nIt is not a provision, and it is not a reserve.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Item) with List-II (Head in Statement of Profit and Loss):\nList-I\n(A) Staff welfare expenses\n(B) Interest on debentures\n(C) Goods purchased for resale\n(D) Dividend received on investments\nList-II\n(i) Finance costs\n(ii) Other income\n(iii) Employee benefits expense\n(iv) Purchases of stock-in-trade","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Staff welfare is an expense on employees, so it goes to Employee benefits expense (iii).\n(B) Interest on borrowings such as debentures is a Finance cost (i).\n(C) Goods bought for trading are Purchases of stock-in-trade (iv).\n(D) Dividend income of a non-finance company is Other income (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Proposed dividend of the previous year, approved by shareholders at the AGM held in the current year, is accounted for as a liability in the current year.\nStatement II: Proposed dividend recommended by the Board for the current year is shown under 'Short-term provisions' in the current year's balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The AGM is held in the next financial year. Once shareholders approve the dividend, it becomes a liability and is accounted for in that year. \nStatement II is incorrect. Until approval, the dividend is contingent, since shareholders may reduce it but cannot increase it. As per AS-4, it is shown in the Notes to Accounts, not as a provision. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The total of 'Reserves and Surplus' in a company's balance sheet may be shown as a negative figure.\nReason (R): After the negative balance of Surplus is adjusted, the resulting balance of Reserves and Surplus is shown under that head even if it is negative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In the Avalon Ltd. illustration, a General Reserve of Rs. 5 lakh against a debit balance of Rs. 8 lakh gives Reserves and Surplus of Rs. (3,00,000).\nR is true. Schedule III states that the balance after adjusting a negative Surplus is shown under Reserves and Surplus even when the result is negative.\nR is the rule that directly produces the situation described in A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Practice followed in preparing financial statements) with List-II (Basis).\nList-I\nA. Assets are shown at historical cost because the enterprise is expected to exist for a long period\nB. Machinery bought in 2010 and in 2024 is shown at the amounts paid, ignoring the change in purchasing power\nC. A sale made on instalments is included in sales of the year of sale, though the price is collected over three years\nD. Provision for doubtful debts is created on the basis of estimates\nList-II\n(i) Money measurement postulate\n(ii) Personal judgement\n(iii) Going concern postulate\n(iv) Realisation postulate","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): The going concern postulate assumes long existence, so assets are shown at historical cost.\nB-(i): The money measurement postulate assumes the value of money remains the same. So assets bought at different times are shown at the amounts paid.\nC-(iv): The realisation postulate includes revenue in the year the sale is made, even if the price is received over years.\nD-(ii): Provisions for doubtful debts rest on estimates, which are personal judgements.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items are shown under Current assets in a company's balance sheet?\nLoose tools; Stores and spares; Goodwill; Capital work-in-progress; Bills receivable (due in 2 months); Cash at bank; Deferred tax assets; Investments to be sold within 6 months; Patents","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Current assets:\n- Loose tools and stores and spares are inventories.\n- Bills receivable are trade receivables.\n- Cash at bank is a cash equivalent.\n- Investments to be sold within 6 months are current investments.\nThat makes 5 items.\nNon-current assets:\n- Goodwill and patents are intangible assets.\n- Capital work-in-progress is a fixed asset.\n- Deferred tax assets are always non-current.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"A company has made a provision for taxation that will be settled within 12 months of the balance sheet date. How is it shown on the face of the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Short-term provisions under Current Liabilities","Long-term provisions under Non-current Liabilities","Reserves and Surplus under Shareholders' Funds","Other current assets under Current Assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A provision to be settled within 12 months of the balance sheet date, or within the operating cycle, is a short-term provision.\nShort-term provisions are shown under Current Liabilities.\nLong-term provisions apply only to provisions settled beyond that period.\nA provision is an obligation, so it can be neither a reserve nor an asset.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"From the following balances of Kaveri Ltd. as at 31 March, find the amount to be shown against the sub-head 'Inventories' in its balance sheet:\nStock-in-trade ₹1,80,000; Loose tools ₹15,000; Stores and spare parts ₹25,000; Bills receivable ₹40,000.","image":"","options":["₹1,80,000","₹1,95,000","₹2,20,000","₹2,60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Stock-in-trade ₹1,80,000; loose tools ₹15,000; stores and spares ₹25,000; bills receivable ₹40,000.\nStock-in-trade, loose tools and stores and spare parts are all classified under Inventories, a current asset.\nBills receivable are Trade receivables, not inventory.\nSubstituting: 1,80,000 + 15,000 + 25,000 = ₹2,20,000.\nSo Inventories = ₹2,20,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A machine with a useful life of only 10 months is shown under current assets in the balance sheet.\nReason (R): In general, an asset expected to be realised within twelve months after the reporting date is classified as current.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: tangible and intangible fixed assets are always non-current. This holds even if their useful life is less than 12 months.\nR is true: the 12-month realisation test is one of the general criteria for classifying an item as current.\nThis general criterion does not apply to fixed assets, which Schedule III treats as non-current.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"During the year, a company paid interest on its term loan and also paid bank charges for maintaining its current account. Under which head of the Statement of Profit and Loss will the bank charges be shown?","image":"","options":["Finance costs","Other expenses","Employee benefits expense","Exceptional items"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under 'Finance costs', only interest on borrowings is shown. \nNCERT states that other financial expenses, such as bank charges, are shown under 'Other expenses'. \n'Finance costs' is the tempting choice, but it is wrong because bank charges are not interest. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Under Schedule III to the Companies Act, 2013, loose tools held by a manufacturing company are shown in the balance sheet under:","image":"","options":["Non-current assets – Fixed assets – Tangible assets","Current assets – Inventories","Current assets – Other current assets","Non-current assets – Other non-current assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Under Schedule III, inventories are always treated as current assets.\nLoose tools are inventory items. In NCERT's illustrations they are shown under Inventories, along with stock-in-trade.\nThey are not tangible fixed assets, even though they are used in production.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Financial statements help investors and creditors predict, compare and evaluate the amount, timing and related uncertainties of a firm's potential cash flows. Which objective of financial statements does this describe?","image":"","options":["To provide information about economic resources and obligations","To judge the effectiveness of management","To provide information about cash flows","To disclose accounting policies"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the cash flow objective as giving investors and creditors useful information for predicting, comparing and evaluating potential cash flows.\nThese cash flows are judged in terms of amount, timing and related uncertainties.\nThe 'economic resources and obligations' objective is about the firm's resources and obligations, not its future cash flows.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"An investor finds that a company's financial statements say nothing about strained labour relations or falling quality of work in its factory. Which limitation of financial statements does this illustrate?","image":"","options":["Bias","Aggregate information","No qualitative information","They are only interim reports"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Financial statements contain only monetary information. \nThey omit qualitative matters such as industrial relations, labour relations and quality of work. This is the limitation 'No qualitative information'. \n'Bias' relates to accountants' personal judgements. \n'Aggregate information' relates to a lack of detail in the monetary figures, not to non-monetary facts. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match the items in List-I with the sub-heads under which they are shown in the balance sheet (List-II):\nList-I\n(a) Goodwill\n(b) Bills receivable\n(c) Stores and spares\n(d) Cash at bank\nList-II\n(i) Inventories\n(ii) Cash and cash equivalents\n(iii) Intangible assets\n(iv) Trade receivables","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Goodwill has no physical form, so it is shown under Fixed assets → Intangible assets: (iii).\n(b) Bills receivable arise from credit sales made in the normal course of business, so they fall under Trade receivables: (iv).\n(c) Stores and spares are inventories, which are always current: (i).\n(d) Cash at bank is shown under Cash and cash equivalents, as defined by AS-3: (ii).\nThe correct match is (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (items) with List-II (sub-heads in the balance sheet as per Schedule III):\nList-I\n(A) Unpaid dividend\n(B) Public deposits (repayable after 3 years)\n(C) Debenture redemption reserve\n(D) Stores and spare parts\nList-II\n(i) Inventories\n(ii) Reserves and surplus\n(iii) Long-term borrowings\n(iv) Other current liabilities","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Unpaid dividend has been declared but not yet claimed. It is payable on demand, so it goes under Other current liabilities → (iv).\n(B) Public deposits repayable after more than 12 months are Long-term borrowings → (iii).\n(C) Debenture redemption reserve is a reserve created out of profits, so it goes under Reserves and surplus → (ii).\n(D) Stores and spare parts are held for consumption, and inventories are always current → (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Kaveri Ltd. has an authorised capital of 1,00,000 equity shares of Rs. 50 each. It issued 60,000 shares, and applications were received for 55,000 shares, all of which were allotted. All calls were made and received, except the final call of Rs. 15 per share on 1,000 shares. The company forfeited 600 of these shares, and they have not been reissued. What amount of share capital will appear on the face of the balance sheet?","image":"","options":["Rs. 27,14,000","Rs. 27,35,000","Rs. 27,50,000","Rs. 27,56,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 55,000 shares subscribed. 1,000 defaulters, of whom 600 were forfeited, so 400 still have calls in arrears.\nSubscribed and fully paid: 54,000 × 50 = Rs. 27,00,000.\nSubscribed but not fully paid: 400 × 50 = Rs. 20,000, less 400 × 15 = Rs. 6,000. This gives Rs. 14,000.\nAdd forfeited shares: 600 × (50 − 15) = Rs. 21,000.\nSo share capital = 27,00,000 + 14,000 + 21,000 = Rs. 27,35,000.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Item) with List-II (Head under which it is shown):\nList-I\n(A) Bank loan repayable after 4 years\n(B) Loan repayable on demand\n(C) Portion of a long-term loan repayable within the next 12 months\n(D) Trade payables to be settled beyond 12 months (beyond operating cycle)\nList-II\n(I) Other current liabilities\n(II) Other long-term liabilities\n(III) Long-term borrowings\n(IV) Short-term borrowings","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) A loan repayable after more than 12 months is a Long-term borrowing (III).\n(B) A loan repayable on demand is a Short-term borrowing (IV).\n(C) The current maturity of a long-term loan is shown under Other current liabilities (I), with a note.\n(D) Trade payables to be settled beyond 12 months go under Other long-term liabilities (II).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. has 20,000 equity shares of Rs. 10 each subscribed and fully called up. The final call of Rs. 3 per share was not received on 600 shares. Of these, 400 shares were forfeited and not reissued. In the notes to accounts, what is the total amount shown under 'Subscribed but not fully paid-up capital', including the share forfeiture amount added?","image":"","options":["Rs. 4,200","Rs. 1,400","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 5,400"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 200 shares remain with calls in arrears of Rs. 3 each. 400 shares are forfeited, and Rs. 7 had been paid on each of them.\nSubscribed but not fully paid: 200 × 10 = Rs. 2,000.\nLess calls in arrears: 200 × 3 = Rs. 600. This leaves Rs. 1,400.\nAdd share forfeiture: 400 × 7 = Rs. 2,800.\nSo the total = 1,400 + 2,800 = Rs. 4,200.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"From the following information of Jam Ltd., find the total of the balance sheet prepared as per Schedule III:\nEquity share capital Rs. 16,00,000; 8% Preference share capital Rs. 6,00,000; General reserve Rs. 6,00,000; 12% Debentures Rs. 12,00,000; Bills payable Rs. 1,50,000; Creditors Rs. 2,00,000; Provision for taxation Rs. 2,50,000; Land and building Rs. 16,00,000; Plant and machinery Rs. 8,00,000; Non-current investments Rs. 10,00,000; Inventories Rs. 7,00,000; Cash at bank Rs. 5,00,000.","image":"","options":["Rs. 46,00,000","Rs. 40,00,000","Rs. 43,50,000","Rs. 52,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Shareholders' funds: Share capital 16,00,000 + 6,00,000 = 22,00,000; Reserves and surplus 6,00,000. Total = 28,00,000.\nNon-current liabilities: Long-term borrowings (12% debentures) = 12,00,000.\nCurrent liabilities: Trade payables (1,50,000 + 2,00,000) = 3,50,000; Short-term provisions = 2,50,000. Total = 6,00,000.\nEquity and liabilities total = 28,00,000 + 12,00,000 + 6,00,000 = 46,00,000.\nCheck with assets: 16,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 10,00,000 + 7,00,000 + 5,00,000 = 46,00,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the financial statements of a company as described in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Balance sheet","Statement of profit and loss","Cash flow statement","Directors' report"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists three financial statements: the balance sheet as at the end of the accounting period, the statement of profit and loss, and the cash flow statement.\nThe directors' report is part of the company's annual report.\nThe annual report also contains the financial statements, but the directors' report is not itself a financial statement.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-COMPANY-FS-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Financial Statements of a Company","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Financial Statements of a Company","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following will appear under 'Current Liabilities' in the balance sheet of a company as per Schedule III?\n(i) Bank loan repayable on demand\n(ii) Instalment of a 5-year term loan falling due within the next 12 months\n(iii) Deferred tax liability\n(iv) Trade payables to be settled within the operating cycle\n(v) Provision for gratuity payable after 3 years\n(vi) Proposed dividend for the current year\n(vii) Interest accrued and due on debentures","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Current: short-term borrowings.\n(ii) Current: the current maturity of long-term debt goes under other current liabilities.\n(iii) Not current: deferred tax is always non-current.\n(iv) Current: trade payables.\n(v) Not current: this is a long-term provision.\n(vi) Not current: it is a contingent liability shown in Notes to Accounts.\n(vii) Current: other current liabilities.\nThe current items are (i), (ii), (iv) and (vii), which makes 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Financial Statements of a Company","difficulty":"medium"}]});
